{"name":"Blue Funk","hex":"#2d4470","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue funk","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A state of nervous depression."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue_funk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue Funk","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Funk is the fourth studio album by American hip hop group Heavy D & the Boyz. It was released on December 22, 1992, via Uptown Records. The recording sessions took place at The Hit Factory, Soundtrack Studios, Axis Studios, Greene St. Recording and D&D Studios in New York. The album was produced by Jesse West, Pete Rock, Tony Dofat, DJ Premier, Skeff Anselm, Steely & Clevie, with DJ Eddie F, Heavy D, and Puff Daddy serving as executive producers. It features guest appearances from 3rd Eye, Busta Rhymes, Gang Starr, Notorious B.I.G., Phat Doug and Rob-O. The album peaked at number 40 on the Billboard 200 and number 7 on the Top R&B/Hip-Hop Albums. It was certified Gold by the Recording Industry Association of America on March 17, 1993, for selling 500,000 units in the US."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/48/Blue_Funk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Funk","wikidata":"Q3641157"},{"title":"Funk","blocks":[{"text":"Funk is a music genre that originated in African-American communities in the mid-1960s when musicians created a rhythmic, danceable new form of music through a mixture of various music genres that were popular among African-Americans in the mid-20th century. It deemphasizes melody and chord progressions and focuses on a strong rhythmic groove of a bassline played by an electric bassist and a drum part played by a percussionist, often at slower tempos than other popular music. Funk typically consists of a complex percussive groove with rhythm instruments playing interlocking grooves that create a \"hypnotic\" and \"danceable\" feel. Early funk, specifically James Brown, fused jazz and blues, and added a syncopated drum groove."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/James-Brown_1973.jpg/330px-James-Brown_1973.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Funk","wikidata":"Q164444"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Meters_Wikepedia.jpg/500px-Meters_Wikepedia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Meters_Wikepedia.jpg/1280px-Meters_Wikepedia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Meters_Wikepedia.jpg/500px-Meters_Wikepedia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The rhythm section of a funk band—the electric bass, drums, electric guitar and keyboards—is the heartbeat of the funk sound. Pictured here are the Meters.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMeters_Wikepedia.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Funk.tiff/lossy-page1-500px-Funk.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Funk.tiff/lossy-page1-500px-Funk.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Simple kick and snare funk motif. The kick first sounds two onbeats, which are then answered by two offbeats. The snare sounds the backbeat.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFunk.tiff"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Thirteenth_chord_collapsed.png/500px-Thirteenth_chord_collapsed.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Thirteenth_chord_collapsed.png/500px-Thirteenth_chord_collapsed.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A thirteenth chord (E 13, which also contains a flat 7th and a 9th) Playⓘ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThirteenth_chord_collapsed.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Bootsy_Collins-04.jpg/500px-Bootsy_Collins-04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Bootsy_Collins-04.jpg/500px-Bootsy_Collins-04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bootsy Collins performing in 1996 with a star-shaped bass","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABootsy_Collins-04.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Cissy_Strut_drum_groove.jpg/500px-Cissy_Strut_drum_groove.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Cissy_Strut_drum_groove.jpg/500px-Cissy_Strut_